# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Root build-context ignore list.
#
# Both `backend/Dockerfile` and `frontend/Dockerfile` are built with the
# REPOSITORY ROOT as the build context, because a pnpm workspace install needs
# `pnpm-lock.yaml` + `pnpm-workspace.yaml` + every workspace `package.json`.
# BuildKit therefore reads THIS file; `backend/.dockerignore` and
# `frontend/.dockerignore` are inert for those builds and are kept only for
# anyone who builds from inside an app directory.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# dependencies (never ship the host's node_modules into the context —
# it is ~1 GB of symlinks and would be re-installed in the image anyway)
**/node_modules
.pnpm-store

# build output
**/dist
**/dev-dist
**/build
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# vcs / editor / os
.git
.github
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db

# secrets & local env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/*.p8
**/*.pem

# infra runtime state — never part of an application image
infra/data
infra/**/certs
backups

# test artefacts
#
# `**/e2e` is the sharp one. It is excluded on purpose — Playwright specs have
# no business in a build image — but `frontend/tsconfig.node.json` typechecks
# `playwright.config.ts`, so ANY import from a build-typechecked file into
# `e2e/` compiles on a developer's machine and dies in the container with
# `TS2307: Cannot find module`. That is exactly how the frontend image broke
# once already. If you need a value shared between the config and the specs,
# put it in the config and let `e2e/` read it back (see `RUN_ID`), never the
# other way round. `infra/scripts/verify-all.sh` now builds both images'
# `build` stage so the next one is caught locally.
**/playwright-report
**/test-results
**/e2e

# docs & tooling that never affect a build
docs
*.md
!frontend/public/**/*.md
Makefile
**/Dockerfile
**/.dockerignore
